Start VNC stream
  Parse the endpoint and negotiate the server version and authentication.
  Read the framebuffer dimensions and validate them.
  Discard the desktop name using the metadata rules below.
  Request the preferred pixel format, encodings and initial full frame.

Handle server messages
  Apply Raw, CopyRect, and Hextile rectangles inside the framebuffer bounds.
  A final DesktopSize rectangle retains overlapping pixels and requests a full frame.
  Keep the last published frame while waiting for pixels at the new size.
  Publish updated pixels and request incremental updates once pixels are available.
  A quiet incremental request may wait indefinitely for changes.
  Ignore bells and discard clipboard or cursor payloads.

Decode a Hextile rectangle
  Decode tiles left to right and top to bottom, using smaller tiles at the edges.
  For a raw tile, copy the decoded pixels and clear both carried colors.
  Otherwise update the supplied colors and fill the tile with its background.
  Apply each subrectangle using its explicit color or the carried foreground.
  Clear the carried foreground when the tile uses explicit subrectangle colors.
  Reject missing required colors, invalid tile bounds, and excessive subrectangle painting.

Discard unused metadata
  Read the unsigned 32-bit text length for a desktop name or clipboard message.
  Reject more than 1 MiB before reading its payload.
  Reject cursor dimensions above 4,096 pixels per side before calculating payload size.
  Discard exactly the declared payload using bounded memory.
  Report oversized metadata and close the stream.
